WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.850
- [Instructor] We're
told that Rodrigo watches

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:04.690
a helicopter take off from a platform.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.760
The height of the helicopter,
in meters above the ground,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.400
t minutes after takeoff is modeled by,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.480
and we see this function right over here.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.260
Rodrigo wants to know when the helicopter

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.040
will land on the ground.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.923
So pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.470
All right, now let's
think about this together.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.390
So let's just imagine
actually what the graph

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.170
of this function looks like.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.230
And it'll also help us
imagine what's going on

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.550
with the helicopter.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.550
So our horizontal axis,
this is t, time in minutes,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.570
and then our vertical axis is height.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.720
So height as a function of time.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.180
And maybe I just write it like this.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.443
I'll just write height,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.850
and this is given in
meters above the ground.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.150
Now I don't know exactly
what the graph looks like,

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.380
but given that I have
a negative coefficient

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.000
on my quadratic term,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.500
I know that it is a downward
opening parabola like that.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.290
And it says that the helicopter
takes off of a platform.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.460
So however high the platform is,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.730
then it takes off and it's
going to do something like this.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.320
I don't know exactly what
the graph looks like,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.440
but probably something like this.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.280
Now, if they asked us
what is the highest point

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.370
of the helicopter, and at
what time does it happen,

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.720
then we'd wanna figure
out what the vertex is

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.160
of this parabola.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.550
But that's not what they're asking.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:36.250
They're asking when does the
helicopter land on the ground?

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.430
That's this time right over here.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:39.730
So if we wanted to find the vertex,

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.760
we would wanna put this into vertex form,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.390
but here we wanna figure out when does

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.110
that function equal zero.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.840
We want to find a zero of this
quadratic right over here.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.160
So the best way that I
can think about doing it

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.630
is try to factor it,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.830
try to set them this thing equal to zero,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.450
and then factor it and
then see what t values

00:01:58.450 --> 00:01:59.610
make that equal to zero.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:00.740
So let me do that.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.740
So I say negative three t
squared plus 24t plus 60,

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.740
remember we care when our height

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.540
is equal to zero, equal zero.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.020
So let's see maybe the
first thing I would do

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.010
just to simplify the second
degree term a little bit.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.920
Let's just divide both
sides by negative three.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:19.870
If we did that,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.290
this would become t squared
24 divided by negative three

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.630
is negative eight, negative eight t.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.290
60 divided by negative
three is negative 20,

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:30.800
and then zero divided by negative three

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.090
is of course still zero.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.830
And now can I think of
two numbers whose product

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.450
is negative 20?

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:38.990
So they would have different signs

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.290
in order to get a negative product,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.163
and who sum is negative eight.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.090
So let's see what about
negative 10 and two,

00:02:48.147 --> 00:02:50.250
and that scene seems to work.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:55.250
So I could write this as t
minus 10, times t plus two

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.210
is equal to zero.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.855
And so in order to make
this expression equals zero,

00:02:59.855 --> 00:03:02.290
either one of these
could be equal to zero.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.180
So either t minus 10 is equal to zero,

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:08.160
or t plus two is equal to zero.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.630
And of course on the left here,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.100
I can add 10 to both sides.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.950
So either t equals 10,

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.670
or I could subtract two
from both sides here,

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.600
t is equal to negative two.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.050
So there's two places where the function

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.400
is equal to zero.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.110
One at time t equals negative two,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:27.240
and one at time t is equal to 10.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.850
Now we're assuming we're
dealing with positive time here.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.950
We don't know what the
helicopter was doing

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.450
before the takeoff.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.160
So we wouldn't really think about this.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.010
So what we really care about is that t

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.290
is equal to 10 minutes.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:43.120
That's when the helicopter
is right over there.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.230
And actually we know at t equals zero,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.510
these two terms become zero.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.150
We know it takes off at 60 meters.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.274
It goes up.

00:03:50.274 --> 00:03:51.750
If we figured out the vertex,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.250
we would know how high it went,

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.080
but then it starts going back down,

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.870
and in 10 minutes after takeoff,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.443
it is back at zero, back on the ground.

